\title[Berggren-generated Pythagorean triples in a box]
{Berggren-Generated Pythagorean Triples in a Box:\\
Exact Counting, Effective $\Theta(H)$ Bounds,\\ and a Visible-Point Bijection}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{22 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
Berggren's three unimodular matrices $B_1,B_2,B_3$ acting on the seed $(3,4,5)$
generate a ternary tree of integer triples. We prove, in effective and fully
explicit form, the following. First, the tree is exactly the set of positive
primitive Pythagorean triples $(a,b,c)$ whose first leg $a$ is odd (closure by
direct algebraic verification, completeness by a descent on the hypotenuse), and
the tree is \emph{free}: distinct words in the generators produce distinct
triples. Second, writing $\Bx(H)$ for the set of tree triples in the box
$[1,H]^3$, we prove
\[
  \frac{H}{100} \le \#\Bx(H) \le \min\bigl(4H,\ (\lfloor\sqrt H\rfloor+1)^2\bigr)
  \qquad (H \ge 5),
\]
so $\#\Bx(H)=\Theta(H)$ and hence $\#\Bx(H)/H^3\to0$: Berggren-generated triples
occupy a vanishing proportion of the $H^3$ integer points of the box. Third, and
in contrast, we prove the \emph{exact} identity $\#\Pp(H) = 2\,\#\Bx(H)$, where
$\Pp(H)$ is the set of all ordered primitive Pythagorean triples in the same
box; equivalently every primitive Pythagorean triple of the box is
Berggren-generated up to swapping its two legs, so the expected proportion
``$1-o(1)$'' is an exact $1$ with no error term. Fourth, we exhibit a bijection
between $\Bx(H)$ and the coprime, opposite-parity lattice points $(n,m)$ with
$0<n<m$, $m^2+n^2\le H$, reducing the count to a visible-point count in a
circular wedge and identifying the conjectural sharp constant
$\#\Bx(H)/H \to 1/(2\pi)$, confirmed numerically to five decimals. Finally we
analyse the growth geometry: $B_2$ is hyperbolic with expansion factor
$3+2\sqrt2$, while $B_3$ is unipotent with quadratic orbit growth, forcing depth
$\Omega(\sqrt H)$ inside the box against a generic depth $\Theta(\log H)$.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{Pythagorean triples, Berggren tree, lattice point counting, visible
lattice points, coprimality density, Gauss circle problem, unipotent orbits,
Euclid parametrisation}

